{
  "timestamp": "2026-07-22T21:59:10Z",
  "title": "Volunteer page + Momentum Fund campaign page",
  "type": "feature",
  "userRequest": "Project context: We need to modernize the https://www.harvardintech.com/ website from the outside. The site was built with a no-code product so we need to faithfully reproduce it using modern technologies and host it as a GitHub pages website. It also needs a CRM that can be run locally or, ideally also in the cloud via GitHub pages as well (protected with a password and/or GitHub OAuth). Can we dig into this?",
  "description": "Turned two nav links that jumped to homepage bands into real pages, and wired the events page to live data. /volunteer carries the volunteer-team pitch, four benefit blocks, and a CMS-editable open-projects grid (empty state is the production default). /donate is the Momentum Fund campaign page built to the Canva mockup: a full-bleed photo hero whose headline greets subscribers by first name via ?name= (the email merge tag supplies it; a hostile value falls back to the generic headline, sanitized so it can't inject markup), a stats band, the story/why-support narrative from Nicole's copy, three gift-pillar cards with inline SVG icon badges, testimonials, and a closing ask. The give button opens a giving-inquiry email until a donation platform exists, switchable via one CMS field. /events now embeds the live Luma calendar inline (cal-KK3JJjJ39Jwt9kI) with a subscribe strip, and shows the 8 real past events pulled from Luma's public API (no upcoming, matching current reality). Added two CMS collections (Volunteer Projects, Testimonials) plus editable page-copy singletons; both new pages appear on the /admin dashboard. Page bodies were extracted into 16 section components (zero raw HTML in page files) and 3 tested pure helpers (resolveGiveHref, openProjects, byOrder/sortByOrder) with 24 new tests. Hero photo uses the Harvard campus background as a placeholder pending the real event photo from Canva. Known follow-ups: the 8,000+ members figure now differs from the homepage's 1,000+; the mobile nav renders every dropdown expanded site-wide (pre-existing); and a brand-new empty content collection can't be scenario-seeded because Astro's glob loader skips zero-file collections at dev-server boot.",
